  "lesson": {
    "slug": "mlc",
    "title": "Console Operations: Understanding & Setting Field Size",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "What \"Field Size\" Means at the Console",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "field size",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is the dimension of the radiation beam at isocenter, defined by the collimator jaws. It's usually described as X \u00d7 Y in centimeters \u2014 for example, a \"10 \u00d7 10\" field is 10 cm wide by 10 cm tall at isocenter."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Getting field size right matters for two reasons that pull in opposite directions:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Coverage:",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " the field must be large enough to cover the target volume with an adequate margin."
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Sparing:",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " the field must be no larger than necessary, or healthy tissue outside the target gets irradiated for no benefit."
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "This lesson covers how field size is set and read at the console, before you practice it yourself in the simulator."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Jaws \u2014 Symmetric vs. Asymmetric",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Field size is created by two pairs of collimator jaws: the "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "X jaws",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (X1/X2) and the "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Y jaws",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (Y1/Y2). Each jaw in a pair can typically move independently."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Symmetric field:",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " both jaws in a pair are equidistant from central axis (e.g., X1 = X2 = 5 cm gives a 10 cm wide field centered on the beam axis)."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Asymmetric (independent) field:",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " the jaws are set to different distances from central axis, shifting the field off-center without moving the isocenter itself \u2014 useful for half-beam blocks or matching adjacent fields."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page2_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A field defined by X1 = 4 cm and X2 = 8 cm (measured from central axis) is:"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Symmetric",
              "Asymmetric"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Since X1 and X2 are different distances from central axis, the field is shifted off-center \u2014 this is an asymmetric (independent jaw) field."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Field Size Is Defined at Isocenter",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A key point that trips up new students: the field size number displayed at the console is always defined "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "at isocenter",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (typically 100 cm from the source), not at the patient's skin surface."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Because the beam diverges from the source, the actual irradiated area is "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "smaller",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " than the stated field size at any distance closer to the source than isocenter, and "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "larger",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "italic"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " at any distance beyond it."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "This is why field size and SSD/SAD are always considered together \u2014 the same jaw settings produce a different footprint on the patient depending on how far the patient's surface or the target is from the source."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page3_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A 10 \u00d7 10 cm field is defined at isocenter (100 cm). At a distance closer to the source than isocenter, the actual beam footprint is:"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Larger than 10 \u00d7 10 cm",
              "Smaller than 10 \u00d7 10 cm",
              "Exactly 10 \u00d7 10 cm"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Because the beam diverges outward from the source, the footprint is smaller than the stated field size at any distance closer than isocenter."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Collimator Rotation vs. Field Size",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The collimator can also "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "rotate",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " around the central axis, independent of the jaw settings. Rotating the collimator rotates the orientation of the rectangular field \u2014 it does not change the field's dimensions."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Field size (how big) and collimator angle (which way it's oriented) are two separate console settings. Students sometimes confuse changing collimator angle with changing field shape \u2014 it only changes orientation."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "For irregularly shaped targets, jaws alone only produce a rectangle. Shaping "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "within",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "italic"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " that rectangle \u2014 blocking corners, following an organ's contour \u2014 is done with a Multi-Leaf Collimator (MLC), which is its own lesson."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
